From 01cd51000b639cdc3ec91bbdd785bce9be0fa680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlanFokCo <alanfok2868@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] aiprof: point the README at the vendored CUPTI alignment
 contract

Upstream cuprof links against the system CUPTI and deliberately vendors no
CUDA libraries, so its README says nothing about which CUPTI a build ends up
needing. Inside AIProf the compiled libcuprof.so is ptrace-injected into
arbitrary target processes, where the CUPTI ABI is tied to the build
machine's CUDA major and a mismatch with the target host's driver silently
SIGSEGVs the injected process.

Add a short note at the top of the README that sends a builder to AIProf's
vendored-CUPTI directory, which documents the alignment contract and how to
rebuild for a different CUDA major. Without it the constraint is discoverable
only from VENDOR.md, which a reader of the vendored README has no reason to
open.
---
 README.md | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 6ae374f..71fda19 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,3 +1,6 @@
+<!-- AIProf-local modification (Apache-2.0 4(b)): this file differs from
+     upstream cuprof. See VENDOR.md and
+     patches/0003-readme-aiprof-cupti-alignment-note.patch. -->
 # cuprof
 
 A minimal CUDA kernel timeline profiler built on
@@ -5,6 +8,21 @@ A minimal CUDA kernel timeline profiler built on
 get a Chrome-trace JSON you can open in `chrome://tracing` or
 [Perfetto](https://ui.perfetto.dev).
 
+> **Building cuprof for use inside AIProf's CollectionFramework?** Read
+> `../../third_party/cupti/README.md` first. AIProf ptrace-injects the
+> compiled `libcuprof.so` into arbitrary target processes; the CUPTI ABI
+> is tied to the build machine's CUDA major, and a mismatch with the
+> target host's CUDA driver silently SIGSEGVs the injected process. The
+> vendored CUPTI directory documents the alignment contract and how to
+> rebuild for a different CUDA major.
+>
+> Note that the "no CUDA libraries are vendored" statement under **Build**
+> below, and the equivalent one in `NOTICE`, describe upstream cuprof as a
+> standalone repository. This copy ships inside AIProf, which *does* vendor
+> `libcupti.so.*` runtimes one level up in `../../third_party/cupti/`, so
+> that injection also works on targets with no CUPTI of their own; see that
+> directory's `NOTICE` for their licence terms.
+
 ```bash
 cuprof run -o trace.json python train.py
 ```
